#![TODO]
// TODO(wado-lang/wado#1527, pre-existing): an indexed-element read
// (`container[i]`, lowered to the
// `index_value` accessor) is treated as a *fresh* value by the value-copy
// inserter (`lower::plan::value_copy::analyze::is_fresh_value` classifies every
// `Call` / `MethodCall` result as fresh), so no defensive copy is inserted. The
// accessor returns storage aliased into the container, so binding, passing, or
// storing the result aliases the container's element — violating value
// semantics ("every value is deeply copied when assigned or passed").
//
// Repro below: `let mut x = outer[0]` aliases `outer`'s element; mutating `x`
// in place then corrupts `outer[0]`. Fails identically on `origin/main`, so it
// is orthogonal to the value-copy-on-escape / call-argument-elision work — it
// needs its own fix on the *insertion* side.
//
// Proper fix: an accessor that returns a projection of a reference parameter
// (`index_value(self: &List, i) -> T { return self.repr[i] }`) leaks an alias
// to the caller, so either the callee must copy on return or the caller must
// copy the result. The cleanest framing reuses the return channel of the
// interprocedural escape analysis (`optimize::escape`): a call whose result
// aliases an argument/receiver is not fresh and must be wrapped. When that lands
// on the insertion side, `value_copy_elide`'s call-argument stripping must keep
// treating a bare `None` source root as non-fresh (already hardened via
// `is_fresh_rvalue`) so it does not re-strip the newly inserted copies.

test "indexed element bound to a local must be an independent copy" {
    let mut outer: List<List<i32>> = [[1, 2]];
    let mut x = outer[0];
    x[0] = 999;
    assert outer[0][0] == 1;
}

__DATA__
{"test": {}}
